tao — keeper's patched copy
===========================

This directory is tao 0.35.3, the window and event-loop layer under Tauri, as
crates.io serves it, with three changes, all on iOS. The first two are on its UIScene path. It is used
through `[patch.crates-io]` in `src-tauri/Cargo.toml`.

  upstream: https://github.com/tauri-apps/tao
  copied from: the crates.io package tao 0.35.3 (checksum
               d1c93047acf68669466a34690ac58cca7010bd1b201e1ec86f1fd0a75d3dd4a9)
  license: Apache-2.0 (LICENSE and LICENSE.spdx, unchanged)

Why the scene path at all: iOS 27 refuses to launch an app that has not adopted
the UIScene lifecycle, so keeper declares a scene manifest (docs/ios.md). Only
an app with that manifest reaches the code below, which is why both bugs went
unnoticed upstream.

The changes
-----------

1. A use-after-free. `src/platform_impl/ios/view.rs`,
   `configuration_for_connecting_scene_session`, returned
   `Retained::as_ptr(&config)` and then dropped `config`, so UIKit received a
   freed `UISceneConfiguration` and crashed in `objc_retain` inside
   `-[UIApplication _connectUISceneFromFBSScene:transitionContext:]` on every
   launch (kalypso, iOS 27.0). It now returns `Retained::autorelease_return`.

2. The launch URL. `src/platform_impl/ios/app_state.rs`, `connect_scene`: the
   first scene's `UISceneConnectionOptions.URLContexts` are emitted as
   `Event::Opened` (read as optional: iOS 27.0 answers nil for a launch
   without a URL, despite the nonnull header); `src/platform_impl/ios/scene.rs`: the parsing that
   `scene:openURLContexts:` already did moves into `urls_of`, shared by both.
   In the scene lifecycle a URL that launched the app arrives only there — not
   in `application:openURL:options:`, not in `scene:openURLContexts:` — and tao
   through 0.37.1 never reads it, so a `keeper://setup?…` code scanned while
   keeper was closed would open keeper and do nothing.

3. The APNs device token (keeper Epic 98, story 98.1). `src/platform_impl/ios/view.rs`,
   `create_delegate_class`: tao's `AppDelegate` declared no
   `application:didRegisterForRemoteNotificationsWithDeviceToken:` and no
   `application:didFailToRegisterForRemoteNotificationsWithError:`, so the token
   `UIApplication.registerForRemoteNotifications` asks for reached nobody. Both are
   added and hand the token's bytes, or the error's description, to two plain function
   pointers the embedder sets once with
   `platform::ios::set_remote_notification_hooks` (`src/platform/ios.rs`; the cell is
   `REMOTE_NOTIFICATION_HOOKS` in `view.rs`). Unset, both methods do nothing. keeper
   sets them in `src-tauri/crates/keeper/src/push_ios.rs` before the event loop runs.
   Swizzling a method onto the runtime class from Swift was the alternative and was
   refused: it would couple silently to the class name `AppDelegate`.

Retiring it
-----------

When upstream tao fixes the first two and gives the app delegate a way to hand over the
APNs token, move `push_ios.rs` to it, then delete the `[patch.crates-io]` table and this
directory, and re-run the cold-launch check in docs/ios.md.
